Only the files needed for the decoder are kept (native/h264dec/sources.txt lists the compiled
ones; the headers are their includes). Everything else in the release was left out.

Changes (all marked "RetroTele h264dec" in the code, full diff in retrotele-h264dec.patch):
- libavcodec/h264.c: optional size limit before a new size is allocated
  (h264dec_size_ok(), only with -DH264DEC_HOOKS); in CODEC_FLAG_LOW_DELAY mode never add
  output delay after POC gaps; keep the rbsp buffers on a size change (use-after-free, fixed
  the same way in later FFmpeg); clear the stale reference lists on a size change; skip motion
  compensation from a missing reference picture.
- libavcodec/h264_cavlc.c: reject corrupt total_zeros / run_before values (out of bounds read).
- libavcodec/h264_ps.c: reject SPS with chroma format other than 4:2:0 or bit depth other than 8.
- libavcodec/h264idct.c: clip with av_clip_uint8() instead of the crop table (no out of bounds
  read on corrupt coefficients).
The configuration (native/h264dec/config/config.h) disables all assembler and every codec
except the H.264 decoder. The build also maps assert() (native/h264dec/config/catch/assert.h) and
abort() to a handler that jumps back to h264dec_decode(), which resets the decoder instead of
ending the process.

Relinking (LGPL 2.1 section 6): the complete source of this library, the modifications, the
build files and the code that uses it are in this repository. To use a modified FFmpeg:
replace the files here, then run native/h264dec/build_h264dec.sh (static library h264dec.lib and
the benchmark) or native/build_native.sh (all RetroTele binaries, including those that link
h264dec.lib), and pys60/build_sis.sh to package them. The PC test (native/h264dec/tests/
conformance.sh) checks a rebuilt decoder against the system ffmpeg.
